Case 4:

The subquery is using old ANSI SQL -89 syntax for joining tables, which is not recommended. However, as far as performance is concerned, there doesn't seem to be a difference between this and the newer SQL-92 style joins.

One possible optimization would be to use JOIN instead of the subquery. Here's how you can do it:

```sql
SELECT 
  wkr.name AS worker,
  txn.type AS worktype,
  txn.payment_type AS paymenttype,
  SUM(txn.output) AS totalkgs_ltrs,
  SUM(txn.over_kgs) AS overkgs_ltrs,
  SUM(txn.names) AS workedhrs,
  SUM(txn.scrap) AS scrap,
  SUM(txn.cash_work_income) AS cashworkincome,
  SUM(txn.pss) AS pss
FROM 
  bpl_daily_transaction_master txn
INNER JOIN res_company comp ON comp.id = txn.bpl_company_id
INNER JOIN bpl_division_n_registration div ON div.id = txn.bpl_division_id
INNER JOIN bpl_estate_n_registration est ON est.id = txn.bpl_estate_id
INNER JOIN bpl_worker wkr ON wkr.id = txn.worker_id
GROUP BY 
  wkr.name, 
  txn.type, 
  txn.payment_type
```

Case 5:

To speed up this query, consider adding a compound index on your table on (appID, score). This way, the WHERE clause will jump directly to your AppID 60, then subsequently to the score 13956 and get only those higher. It doesn't have to go back to the raw data pages to qualify since you are doing a count(*).

Here's how you can create this index:

```sql
CREATE INDEX idx_appid_score ON users_scores (appID, score);
```

This should help improve query performance by reducing the number of rows that need to be scanned.
